WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
A passing shock can compress a cold cloud.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.040
Dense fragments can then collapse under gravity. The

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.180
shock is a trigger, not a guarantee that

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.500
every cloud becomes a star.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.780
As a protostar contracts, gravitational energy becomes heat

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.180
and radiation. The Kelvin Helmholtz time is proportional

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.440
to mass squared, divided by radius times luminosity.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.520
Hold mass and radius fixed: twice the luminosity

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.660
gives half this characteristic time.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.120
On the main sequence, hydrogen fusion supplies the

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.740
energy. The approximate lifetime instead scales as mass

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.780
divided by luminosity. Massive stars have more fuel,

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.080
but their much greater luminosity uses it faster.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.240
Keep these two stellar clocks separate.
